<details class="debrief">
  <summary>What this exercise is showing</summary>

  <p>The broken dashboard carries its entire meaning in hue. There is no text, no shape, no position and no label &mdash; so the moment hue is unavailable, the information is gone. That happens for around 1 in 12 men and 1 in 200 women with a colour vision deficiency, for anyone using a screen reader, on a printout, on a cheap projector in a meeting room, on a phone in sunlight, and for anyone reading the number out over the phone.</p>

  <p>Notice what the key does. It lists three coloured dots and never says what any of them mean, which is a surprisingly common pattern once you start looking for it.</p>

  <p>There is a second, quieter failure. Run a screen reader over the broken table and the status column is silent: an empty cell in every row. Colour is not just hard to perceive, it is genuinely absent from the accessibility tree.</p>

  <h3>Questions for the group</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Did anyone's count change between the settings? Did anyone feel confident and still get it wrong?</li>
    <li>Where do we use red and green together &mdash; validation, charts, status pills, diffs, availability calendars?</li>
    <li>If someone read our dashboard down the phone to a colleague, what would they lose?</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>What good looks like</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Colour is the second signal, never the first. Add the word, then colour it.</li>
    <li>Where labels will not fit, use shape as well as hue &mdash; a tick, a cross, a warning triangle &mdash; and give the icon a text alternative.</li>
    <li>Charts get direct labels or patterns, not a colour key off to one side.</li>
    <li>Test it by taking a screenshot and desaturating it. If you cannot read it, neither can a good number of your users.</li>
  </ul>
</details>
